(iv)
every power-driven vessel which is under sail and not under power is to be considered a sailing vessel, and every
vessel under power, whether under sail or not, is to be considered a power-driven vessel;
(v)
a vessel or seaplane on the water is "under way" when she is not at anchor, or made fast to the shore, or aground;
(vi)
the term "height above the hull" means height above the uppermost continuous deck;
(vii)
the length the breadth of a vessel shall be deemed to be the length and breadth appearing in her certificate of registry;
(viii)
the length and span of a seaplane shall be its maximum length and span as shown in its certificate of airworthiness, or
as determined by measurement in the absence of such certificate;
(ix)
the word "visible", when applied to lights, means-visible on a dark night with a clear atmosphere;
(x)
the term "short blast" means a blast of about one second's duration;
(xi)
the term "prolonged blast" means a blast of from four to six second's duration.
(xii)
the word "whistle" means whistle or siren;
(xiii)
the word "tons" means gross tons.
